# openFutVE — Data License and Provenance

The **code** in this repository is licensed under Apache-2.0 (see `LICENSE`).
This file covers **data**: what we publish, under what terms, and where it came from.

This is not legal advice, and it is a living document. If you believe a dataset here is
mislabeled or should not be published, open an issue and we will act on it.

---

## 1. The position, in short

Individual football facts (a final score, a match date) are generally not copyrightable.
A *compiled database* of them can attract rights of its own in some jurisdictions, and the
sources we compile from carry their own terms. So we split the question:

| Layer | Terms |
|-------|-------|
| The pipeline (all code in this repo) | Apache-2.0 |
| The **openFutVE compiled database** — our schema, our entity resolution, our aggregates | **ODbL 1.0** |
| Content derived from Wikipedia | **CC BY-SA 4.0**, tracked separately (§3) |
| Third-party API responses | Governed by that provider's ToS — **not** relicensed by us (§3) |

When in doubt, we publish the pipeline rather than the data, and let you materialize your
own copy. That is the strongest legal position available to us, and it is why self-hosting
is a first-class path in this project rather than an afterthought.

## 2. The compiled database (ODbL 1.0)

Data we compiled, cleaned, resolved and derived — the openFutVE database as served by our
API and published in our exports — is made available under the
[Open Database License v1.0](https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/).

You are free to share, create from, and adapt it, provided you:

- **attribute** openFutVE and link to this repository,
- **share alike** — distribute adapted databases under ODbL, and
- **keep it open** — do not use technical measures that restrict others' use.

Attribution string:

> Contains data from openFutVE (https://github.com/openfutve/openfutve), used under ODbL 1.0.

Note that ODbL covers the *database*. Individual factual records extracted from it are not
themselves claimed as copyrightable by this project.

## 3. Upstream sources

Every row in the database carries `source`, `fetched_at` and `confidence` columns, so the
provenance of any single fact is queryable, not guessed. The per-source audit — URL, terms
of service, rate limits, fields, and licensing notes — lives in
[`docs/data-sources.md`](docs/data-sources.md), which is the authoritative record.

Summary of the constraints that shape what we can publish:

- **Wikipedia / Wikidata** — text and structured content are CC BY-SA 4.0 (Wikidata claims
  are CC0). Content derived from Wikipedia is tagged in the database and must be attributed
  and share-aliked accordingly. This is the reason Wikipedia-derived material is tracked
  separately rather than folded into the ODbL pool.
- **TheSportsDB** — accessed under its published API terms. Raw responses are retained
  internally for replay but are **not** republished verbatim; we publish normalized facts
  and derived aggregates only.
- **Sportmonks / API-Football** — free-tier terms typically restrict redistribution. Where
  a provider's terms forbid republication, we use the source for verification and
  cross-checking only, and say so in `docs/data-sources.md`.
- **ligafutve.com / FVF** — official league and federation pages. Scraped subject to
  `robots.txt` and rate limits, with the bot identified in `User-Agent`. Factual results
  are published; page text, images and logos are not.

Where two sources disagree, the database records both origins and our resolution; it does
not silently pick a winner.

## 4. What we never publish

- Verbatim copies of any provider's API payloads.
- Any source's images, logos, crests or editorial text.
- Personal data about players beyond what is routinely published in match reports
  (name, position, appearances, and match events).

## 5. Takedown

Open an issue titled `data: takedown request` or contact the maintainers. We will remove
disputed data while the question is being worked out, not after.
